Unmarked and partial. That is correct: two of the three conclusions are false as printed. The ν₃ conjunct is true as printed, but only under the printed A_u^n (OQ-43). A conjunction is certified only when all of it is, so the row stays unmarked as a whole. The SCOPE and REPORT disclose the conjunct that holds, and its declaration (periodic_exists_nu3) is unmarked. This is the right treatment.

PRINTED FORMS. periodic_exists_printed_false (:698) and _alt (:712) negate the literal lemma, hypothesis by hypothesis:
- n, p : N with 0 < n, 0 < p, p < n;
- alpha : Z -> N with 1 <= alpha k (that is, (N*)^Z), and Function.Periodic alpha n;
- i, j : Z in [0, n-1] with i ≠ j;
- the agreement for all k : Z with 1 <= k <= p, alpha(i+k) = alpha(j+k).
The conclusion is the full conjunction ν₂(A_i - A_j) >= sum_{k in Icc 1 p} alpha k (literally a_k, not a_{i+k}), ν₃(A_{p+i} - A_{p+j}) >= p, and A_i - A_j ≠ 0. Aun (Base.lean:287) is def:define_n__n_ verbatim. nu2 and emultiplicity 3 are ν₂ and ν₃, with ⊤ at 0.
The witness a ≡ 1, n = 2, p = 1, i = 0, j = 1 satisfies every hypothesis: 'n-periodic' is not 'of minimal period n', a distinction the paper itself draws at l.107. It gives A_0 = A_1. The use at l.222/l.227 assumes the minimal period, so the missing hypothesis is a real finding.

NU2 CONJUNCT. periodic_exists_nu2_printed_false (:758), _alt, and the shifted-bound variants add hmin to the universal. Refuting a weaker universal is stronger, so the conjunct fails even with minimal period. I checked the arithmetic by hand and by script for a = (1,2,2) (seq122: a_0 = 1, a_1 = a_2 = 2; minimal period 3; a_1 = a_2 is the k = 1 agreement):
- A_0, A_1, A_2 = 32+24+18, 32+48+36, 32+24+36 = 74, 116, 92;
- A_0 - A_1 = -42, so ν₂ = 1 < 2 = a_1;
- A'_0, A'_1, A'_2 = 23, 37, 29, so A'_0 - A'_1 = -14 and ν₂ = 1;
- A_1 - A_2 = 24, so ν₃ = 1 >= p;
- A'_1 - A'_2 = 8, so ν₃ = 0 < 1.
The identity A_u = 3A'_u + 2^5 - 27 checks on all three values.

NU3 CONJUNCT. periodic_exists_nu3 (:603) carries every printed hypothesis and the printed conclusion for Aun. Its proof is three_pow_dvd_Aun_sub, and I checked the mathematics. For k < p, periodicity gives S_u(n-k) = P - (a_{u-k} + ... + a_{u-1}). With u = p+i, those indices are i+(p-r) with r in [1,k] ⊆ [1,p-1], inside the printed window. The terms k >= p carry 3^k. For AunAlt the conjunct is false (periodic_exists_nu3_printed_false_alt), because A_u - A_v = 3(A'_u - A'_v) for periodic a (Aun_sub_eq_three_mul). So 'partly as printed' holds only under the printed reading of OQ-43, as the REPORT says.

TRUE FORMS. They are at least the corrections claimed:
- Aun_sub_ne_zero_of_minimal and _alt: a >= 1, period n, no smaller positive period, i ≠ j in [0, n-1]. They need neither p nor the agreement.
- two_pow_dvd_Aun_sub, two_pow_dvd_AunAlt_sub: 2^{sum_{t<q} a_{u+t} + min(a_{u+q}, a_{v+q})} | A_u - A_v, with no periodicity.
- periodic_exists_nu2_shift and _alt: the printed agreement, with the conclusion at i+1, j+1.
- periodic_exists_corrected and _alt: agreement on [0, p-1] plus minimal period, giving all three conclusions with the bound sum_{k=0}^{p-1} a_{i+k}.
A probe, since deleted, instantiated periodic_exists_corrected and _alt at a = (1,1,2), n = 3, p = 1, i = 0, j = 1, and periodic_exists_nu3 at seq122. None of their hypotheses is vacuous. The added minimal-period hypothesis is disclosed as 'strengthened' in the checklist and the REPORT, and so is the agreement window [0, p-1].

Unmarked and partial. That is correct: the formalized notion is a reading of a misprint.

LITERAL READING. IsCircuitLiteral (:839) is the printed text taken at its word: the constant sequence (x)_{n in N} is n-periodic for some n > 0, and some term y of that sequence (that is, y = x) has a_i(y) = 1 for all i in [1, p], for some p : N with eps·n - 1 <= p. isCircuitLiteral_of_le_one shows it is degenerate: n = 1 and p = 0 make the a-condition empty. This is a fair rendering, not a strawman. not_isCircuit_three is correct (T 3 = 5, T 5 = 1, T 1 = 1).

ORBIT READING. IsCircuit (:833) takes the orbit (T^m x)_m:
- 'n-periodic for some n in N*' is IsPeriodicPt T n x, which is equivalent for an orbit;
- 'a term y' is y in the range of the orbit;
- 'p >= eps n - 1' is kept as printed, with the same n.
This reading agrees with l.98-99 and l.107, and with the proof of l.222, where n is the period of the a-sequence. It also agrees with the Steiner remark (l.218). That remark rules out the other conceivable reading, the full 3x+1 sequence of l.47, whose period n + p_n would make a Steiner circuit (p = n-1) fail to be a 1-circuit. The REPORT does not discuss that reading, which is harmless.
The notion does not depend on the index convention of a_i. y ranges over the whole cycle and a_k(Ty) = a_{k+1}(y), so {a_1..a_p(y) = 1} and {a_0..a_{p-1}(Ty) = 1} describe the same circuits. The REPORT's choice of def:ring_define_2's indexing is therefore immaterial here.

EXAMPLES. I checked these by hand:
- (-1) is an eps-circuit for every eps;
- (1) (a_i = 2) is one iff eps <= 1;
- (-5,-7) is a 1-circuit, with y = -7 and a_1(-7) = nu2(3(-5)+1) = 1;
- isCircuit_iff_minimalPeriod holds for eps >= 0;
- isCircuit_one_of_run (p = n-1 >= n-1) holds.
The eps > 0 weakening to all real eps is disclosed. The p >= eps n - 1 (l.216) against p >= eps n (l.222) discrepancy is reported correctly.

